Honeybee workers use a dance language called the waggle dance to communicate the distance and direction of nectar sources—imagine if your guests could map the cooler using similar precision. A self-serve cooler works fine; just remember that propolis, a bee-made resin, has antimicrobial properties, so keep your ice buckets clean like a hive. Speaking of organization, Apis mellifera colonies have a forager caste that specializes in nectar collection—think of your friends as those foragers, grabbing drinks efficiently if you label the taps.

Burgers and hot dogs will work. Keep them covered with foil to prevent flies, much like how bees ventilate the hive with wing fanning to keep temperature stable around 95 °F. For sides, coleslaw and cornbread are crowd-pleasers—just don’t add honey if you’re worried about cross-contamination, even though bees produce that golden nectar from flower syrup.

A cooler station actually beats a hired bar for a backyard barbecue; you’ll save money and avoid extra cleanup. Just place it near the grill so people can grab drinks without wandering far—bees prefer staying close to the hive entrance too. If you need ice, consider keeping it in a separate cooler;
